8
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.460
A polynomial many-one reduction computes

9
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.800
a transformation f in polynomial

10
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.240
time. For every source instance

11
00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.440
x, x must be a

12
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.140
yes-instance of A exactly when

13
00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.220
f of x is a

14
00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.240
yes-instance of B.

15
00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.700
Imagine being given an algorithm

16
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.380
that solves B. To solve

17
00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.360
A on input x, first

18
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.160
transform x into f of

19
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.640
x, then run the B

20
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.580
solver and return its yes-or-no

21
00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:40.000
answer.

22
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.240
This means A is no

23
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.620
harder than B under the

24
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.340
reduction, or equivalently B is

25
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.600
at least as hard as

26
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.280
A. The arrow points toward

27
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.320
the solver we would like

28
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.520
to borrow, not toward the

29
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.140
problem we are initially trying

30
00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:54.800
to solve.

31
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:56.780
If B has a polynomial

32
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.160
algorithm, combining it with the

33
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.800
polynomial transformation gives a polynomial

34
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.540
algorithm for A. The transformed

35
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.120
instance has polynomial length because

36
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:10.620
a polynomial-time transformation cannot explicitly

37
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.760
